¶ speaking there is only one hannonic scale, that is, a scale which ¶ the musician to produce chords without beats, and therefore has notes with ¶ numbers composed of products and multiples of the powers of 2, 3, 5, 7, 17, ¶ shewn in Sect. E. But the term harmonic may be extended to all tempered ¶ of such- scales as are not worse than equal intonation. If we did not ¶ the use of the term thus far, we should find absolutely no harmonic scale in ¶ use, except by the Tonic Sol-faists when unaccompanied (App. XVIII.). ¶ with this extension of meaning, non-harmonic scales are greatly more ¶ than harmonic. Harmony was a European discovery of a fcAV centuries ¶ and it has not penetrated beyond Europe and its colonies.
